1
00:00:00,190 --> 00:00:00,800
Click.

2
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:00,800
Click.

3
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:03,400
You’ve done it a thousand times without thinking.

4
00:00:03,150 --> 00:00:05,349
But inside that little click is one

5
00:00:05,099 --> 00:00:07,243
of the cleverest machines you own.

6
00:00:07,743 --> 00:00:10,113
This is the humble retractable ballpoint.

7
00:00:09,863 --> 00:00:11,743
A barrel, a button and a tip.

8
00:00:11,493 --> 00:00:14,383
Most of us never give it a second thought.

9
00:00:14,133 --> 00:00:15,589
But unscrew the barrel,

10
00:00:15,339 --> 00:00:17,318
and it comes apart into a handful

11
00:00:17,068 --> 00:00:18,785
of beautifully simple parts.

12
00:00:20,456 --> 00:00:21,671
Laid out in a line,

13
00:00:21,421 --> 00:00:22,890
there’s the push button,

14
00:00:22,640 --> 00:00:24,008
a little rotating cam,

15
00:00:23,758 --> 00:00:25,379
the spring, the ink refill,

16
00:00:25,129 --> 00:00:27,056
and the two halves of the barrel.

17
00:00:26,806 --> 00:00:27,867
Just a few parts,

18
00:00:27,617 --> 00:00:29,156
and not one of them wasted.

19
00:00:28,906 --> 00:00:31,041
Every one of them has exactly one

20
00:00:30,791 --> 00:00:31,612
job to do.

21
00:00:32,662 --> 00:00:34,092
The magic is in the cam.

22
00:00:33,842 --> 00:00:35,821
Inside the barrel run little ridges,

23
00:00:35,571 --> 00:00:37,789
with deep channels between them and short

24
00:00:37,539 --> 00:00:38,462
ledges on top.

25
00:00:38,212 --> 00:00:39,294
Press the button,

26
00:00:39,044 --> 00:00:40,909
and its angled teeth push the cam

27
00:00:40,659 --> 00:00:41,839
down, and twist it,

28
00:00:41,589 --> 00:00:42,622
just a fraction.

29
00:00:42,372 --> 00:00:44,702
Let go, and the cam lands on a ledge.

30
00:00:44,452 --> 00:00:45,667
The tip is held out,

31
00:00:45,417 --> 00:00:46,392
ready to write.

32
00:00:46,142 --> 00:00:48,474
Press again, and it turns a little further,

33
00:00:48,224 --> 00:00:49,733
slips into a deep channel,

34
00:00:49,483 --> 00:00:51,621
and the spring pulls the tip back home.

35
00:00:52,591 --> 00:00:54,551
So one button does two jobs.

36
00:00:54,301 --> 00:00:56,190
The spring is always pushing back,

37
00:00:55,940 --> 00:00:58,551
and the cam remembers where it’s allowed to rest.

38
00:00:58,301 --> 00:01:00,228
Squeezed a little with every click,

39
00:00:59,978 --> 00:01:02,097
the spring gives the button its springy

40
00:01:01,847 --> 00:01:03,822
feel, and it’s strong enough to pull

41
00:01:03,572 --> 00:01:05,738
the tip home the moment the cam lets go.

42
00:01:06,088 --> 00:01:08,428
The refill itself is wonderfully simple.

43
00:01:08,178 --> 00:01:09,453
A thin tube of ink,

44
00:01:09,203 --> 00:01:11,718
with the writing tip pressed onto the end.

45
00:01:11,468 --> 00:01:12,651
When it runs dry,

46
00:01:12,401 --> 00:01:13,529
you swap it out,

47
00:01:13,279 --> 00:01:15,229
and the rest of the pen carries

48
00:01:14,979 --> 00:01:16,436
on, click after click.

49
00:01:17,426 --> 00:01:18,566
Now, the tip.

50
00:01:18,316 --> 00:01:20,178
This is the clever part,

51
00:01:19,928 --> 00:01:22,326
patented by László Bíró in 1938.

52
00:01:22,076 --> 00:01:24,796
Bíró was a newspaper editor in Budapest.

53
00:01:24,546 --> 00:01:27,163
He noticed that newspaper ink dried quickly,

54
00:01:26,913 --> 00:01:28,669
but it was far too thick for

55
00:01:28,419 --> 00:01:29,476
a fountain pen.

56
00:01:29,226 --> 00:01:31,734
His answer was a tiny rolling ball.

57
00:01:31,664 --> 00:01:33,671
At the very end sits that ball,

58
00:01:33,421 --> 00:01:35,882
often just seven tenths of a millimetre

59
00:01:35,632 --> 00:01:38,263
across, usually tungsten carbide or steel,

60
00:01:38,013 --> 00:01:40,700
held in a socket just loose enough to spin.

61
00:01:41,050 --> 00:01:42,750
As you write, the ball rolls.

62
00:01:42,500 --> 00:01:43,942
Its top dips into thick,

63
00:01:43,692 --> 00:01:45,979
oil-based ink, and its bottom lays a thin

64
00:01:45,729 --> 00:01:47,270
film of it onto the paper.

65
00:01:47,020 --> 00:01:48,220
The ink is a paste,

66
00:01:47,970 --> 00:01:49,120
so it doesn’t run,

67
00:01:48,870 --> 00:01:51,770
and fatty acids in it keep the ball turning smoothly.

68
00:01:51,520 --> 00:01:53,648
Most ballpoints rely on gravity too,

69
00:01:53,398 --> 00:01:55,840
which is why they won’t write upside down.

70
00:01:55,590 --> 00:01:57,082
And when you stop writing,

71
00:01:56,832 --> 00:01:58,754
the ball sits snugly in its socket,

72
00:01:58,504 --> 00:02:00,522
helping keep the ink from drying out.

73
00:02:01,262 --> 00:02:02,772
Put it all back together.

74
00:02:02,522 --> 00:02:03,826
The button presses,

75
00:02:03,576 --> 00:02:04,603
the cam turns,

76
00:02:04,353 --> 00:02:05,657
the spring returns,

77
00:02:05,407 --> 00:02:06,712
and the ball rolls.

78
00:02:06,462 --> 00:02:08,046
A complete little machine,

79
00:02:07,796 --> 00:02:09,226
sitting in your pocket.

80
00:02:09,785 --> 00:02:11,765
A spring, held back by a catch,

81
00:02:11,515 --> 00:02:13,215
let go one step at a time.

82
00:02:12,965 --> 00:02:16,449
Now picture that same idea eight times every second,

83
00:02:16,199 --> 00:02:18,065
for years, inside a Rolex.

84
00:02:17,815 --> 00:02:20,020
That one’s linked right below.

